/// Chapter 15 of the guide. This page is its source: edit it here.
page Learn15Data(path: "/learn/15-data", title: "Fetching data", description: "Show a request with resource and match, ask with http in an action, describe a service once with api, handle each failure, and read data files.", layout: DocsShell("Fetching data", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.build"), blurb: "Get data onto a page: show a request's cases, ask from an action, describe a service once, and read files at build time.", source: "site/src/pages/guide/15-data.wf", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "ways-to-get-data", label: "Ways to get data"), Anchor(id: "resources", label: "Resources"), Anchor(id: "refreshing", label: "Refreshing"), Anchor(id: "one-off-requests-with-http", label: "One-off requests with http"), Anchor(id: "api-services", label: "API services"), Anchor(id: "error-enums", label: "Error enums"), Anchor(id: "writes", label: "Writes"), Anchor(id: "pagination-and-streaming", label: "Pagination and streaming"), Anchor(id: "credentials-and-middleware", label: "Credentials and middleware"), Anchor(id: "openapi", label: "OpenAPI"), Anchor(id: "testing-with-fakes", label: "Testing with fakes"), Anchor(id: "offline-writes", label: "Offline writes"), Anchor(id: "build-time-data", label: "Build-time data"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting"), Anchor(id: "quiz", label: "Quiz"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Show a read's three cases — `.loading`, `.failed(e)`, `.loaded(value)` — with `resource` and `match`", "Tell a refresh (`refreshing`, the value stays) from a first load (`.loading`)", "Ask on demand with `http` in an action, and handle its result with `match` or `try`", "Describe a service once with `api`, and say how each call fails with an error enum", "Make a write refresh the reads it changes, and show a change before the server agrees", "Test a page against a `fake`, and read a JSON file at build time with `data`"], prevTo: "/learn/14-forms", prevLabel: t("ch.14"), nextTo: "/learn/16-testing", nextLabel: t("ch.16"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter is about getting data onto a WebFluent page and sending data back. It starts with a single request and grows to a whole service.\n\nYou will be able to:\n\n- show a request's `.loading`, `.failed(e)` and `.loaded(value)` cases with `resource` and `match`;\n- refresh data without the page going blank;\n- ask from an action with `http`, and handle what comes back;\n- describe a service once with `api`, and say how each call fails;\n- make a write refresh what it changed, page through long answers, and test a page without a server;\n- read a JSON file at build time and render one page per item in it.\n\nEvery option this chapter mentions, and the ones it leaves out, is in [Services and requests](/reference/requests).")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You should know `state` and `action` ([State and reactivity](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity)), `match` ([Control flow](/learn/09-control-flow)) and how to declare a `type` ([Types](/learn/12-types)).\n\nThis chapter does not cover:\n\n- a connection the page holds open — a socket, a stream, a channel or a peer — which is [Open a socket](/recipes/open-a-socket);\n- values fixed for each build, which are [Environments and secrets](/recipes/environments-and-secrets).")

        Heading("Ways to get data", id: "ways-to-get-data").h2

        Table(caption: "Choose how to get data") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("You want…").header
                    Table.Cell("Use").header
                    Table.Cell("Section").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Content known before the site is built")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`data`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Read a file at build time](#build-time-data)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Data the page shows as soon as it opens")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`resource` over `http.get(…)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Show a request](#resources)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A request made when something happens: a save, a delete")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`http.post(…)` in an action") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Ask on demand](#one-off-requests-with-http)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("One place that describes a service, used from many pages")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`api`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Describe a service](#api-services)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Start with `http`. Move to `api` when the same service is called from more than one place: the address, the types and the failures are then written once.")

        Heading("Resources", id: "resources").h2

        Markdown("A *resource* is a request the page makes when it shows, and one value that says where the request is: `.loading`, `.failed(e)` or `.loaded(value)`. Show it with `match`.\n\nThis page lists recent deploys, with a spinner while they load and an alert if the request fails:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Deploy { id: String, status: String, startedAt: String }

page Deploys(path: "/deploys", title: "Deploys", description: "Recent deploys.") {
    resource deploys: [Deploy] = http.get("/api/deploys")

    Heading("Deploys").h1
    match deploys {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert("Could not load: {e.message}").danger }
        .loaded(rows) {
            if rows.length == 0 { Text("No deploys yet.").muted }
            for d in rows by d.id { Text("{d.id}: {d.status}, {ago(d.startedAt)}") }
        }
    }
    Button("Refresh") { on click { deploys.reload() } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        DocsCallout("**Coming from React?** There, a fetch is a `useEffect` that sets `loading`, `error` and `data` states by hand, or a `useQuery`. A `resource` is the request and its state together: no effect, no flags to keep in step, and a `match` with one arm per case. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react).", kind: .note)

        Markdown("How it works:\n\n- `resource name: T = http.get(url)` starts the request when the page renders, and cancels it when the page leaves.\n- The annotation, `: [Deploy]`, says what the answer is. A read of `http.get` must have one (`N11`). The answer is checked against it in every build: a server that sends another shape fails as `.decode(at, message)`, `$[1].status is missing`.\n- The three cases:\n\n  | Case | When | |---|---| | `.loading` | nothing to show yet | | `.failed(e)` | the first load failed; `e` is an `HttpError` | | `.loaded(value, refreshing, stale)` | something to show; `refreshing` while it is asked again; `stale` the failure of a refresh, else `null` |\n\n  An arm names as many of its case's places as it uses: `.loaded(rows)`. A `match` with no `.failed` arm and no `else` is `T21`; one that leaves out another case is `T15`.\n- `e.message` is something you can show. For a failed status it is the server's own words when its body has them — its `detail`, else its `title` — and `HTTP 500` when it says nothing.\n- A static build's pre-rendered page shows the `.loading` arm.")

        Heading("Resource members", id: "resource-members").h3

        Markdown("Without a `match`, a resource has members worked out from its one value. Each is live wherever you read it:")

        Table(caption: "Read one fact of a resource anywhere") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Member").header
                    Table.Cell("What it is").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.value`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The value while it is `.loaded`, else `null`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.isLoading` · `name.isLoaded` · `name.isFailed`") }
                    Table.Cell("Which case it is in")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.isRefreshing`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true` while a refresh runs") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.error`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The first load's failure, or the last refresh's, else `null`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.pending`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true` while any request of it runs, the first load included") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`name.reload()`") }
                    Table.Cell("Asks again")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("So `Button(if plan.isLoaded \{ \"Request approval\" \} else \{ \"Deploy\" \})` changes its label when the plan arrives, and `if let .failed(e) = plan \{ Alert(e.message).danger \}` shows one case without a `match`.")

        Heading("Refetching on state change", id: "refetching-on-state-change").h3

        Markdown("An address that reads state is asked again whenever that state changes. A detail page follows its route parameter this way:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, body: String }

page PostPage(path: "/posts/:slug", title: "Post", description: "One post.", slug: String) {
    resource post: Post = http.get("/api/posts/{slug}")
    match post {
        .loading { Skeleton }
        .failed(e) { Alert("Not found").warning }
        .loaded(p) {
            Heading(p.title).h1
            Markdown(p.body)
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A value spliced into the address is URL-encoded where it stands for a value. The same holds for a body or a header that reads state. To ask less often than the reader types, splice a slower value, as [Debounce a search](/recipes/debounce-a-search) does.")

        Heading("Refreshing", id: "refreshing").h2

        Markdown("Asking again for the request already shown is a *refresh*: `name.reload()` asks, and so does `refresh:` on the request — `.onReconnect` (what a read does unless it says otherwise), `.onFocus`, `.every(30.seconds)` or `.never`. While a refresh runs, the read stays `.loaded` with its value, and `refreshing` is true. This page polls every 30 seconds:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Server { id: String, name: String, status: String }

page Servers(path: "/servers", title: "Servers", description: "Every server, kept current.") {
    state stage = "test"
    resource servers: [Server] = http.get("/api/servers?env={stage}", refresh: .every(30.seconds))

    Heading("Servers").h1
    Button("Production") { on click { stage = "prod" } }
    match servers {
        .loading { Skeleton(height: "20px") }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list, refreshing, stale) {
            if stale != null { Alert("Could not refresh; this is what was there.").warning }
            if refreshing { Spinner.sm }
            for s in list by s.id { Text("{s.name}: {s.status}") }
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The `.loaded` arm is drawn again only when the case changes; inside it, the new value is drawn in place. A row whose key is still in the list keeps its nodes, its focus and what was typed into it, and an answer that changed nothing redraws nothing.\n\nWhen a request fails or changes:\n\n- A refresh that fails stays `.loaded` with the old value, and its failure is `stale` until the next answer.\n- A first load that fails is `.failed(e)`.\n- A request whose address now names something else is a first load again, shown as `.loading`, because the old value would be the wrong one. Pressing **Production** above does this. `placeholder: .previous` keeps the last value shown instead, refreshing, until the new answer arrives — for a search whose results stay while the next ones load.")

        Heading("One-off requests with http", id: "one-off-requests-with-http").h2

        Markdown("For a request made when something happens — a save, a delete, a step in a flow — call `http` in an action: `http.get(url)`, `http.post(url, body: …)`, `http.put`, `http.patch`, `http.delete`. Its answer is a *result*: `.ok(value)`, or `.err(e)` with an `HttpError`. A failure is a value you handle, not an exception you catch.\n\nThis page loads todos and adds one, each with a request:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String, done: Bool = false }
type NewTodo { title: String }

page Todos(path: "/todos", title: "Todos", description: "Todos saved to a server.") {
    state todos: [Todo] = []
    state draft = ""
    state error = ""

    action load() -> ! {
        todos = try await http.get("/api/todos")
    }

    action add() {
        if draft.trim() == "" { return }
        let saved: Todo! = await http.post("/api/todos", body: NewTodo(title: draft))
        match saved {
            .ok(created) {
                todos.push(created)
                draft = ""
            }
            .err(e) { error = "Could not save: {e.message}" }
        }
    }

    load()
    Heading("Todos").h1
    Input(bind: draft, label: "New todo") { on key("Enter") { add() } }
    Button("Add", disabled: add.pending).primary { on click { add() } }
    if error != "" { Alert(error).danger }
    for todo in todos by todo.id { Text(todo.title) }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **The type is named where the answer goes.** `todos = try await http.get(…)` is a `[Todo]` because `todos` is; `let saved: Todo!` names a whole result. A `get` whose answer goes nowhere that says what it is is `N11`.\n- **`match`** gives each case an arm, as `add` does. **`try`** gives the value, or ends the action with the failure as its own, so `load` says what it fails with: `-> !` is nothing, or an `HttpError`.\n- **Options** follow the address by name: `body:` (sent as JSON), `headers:`, `timeout:`, `retry:`, `cache:`, `as: .text`.\n- `load()` at the top of the page runs once, when the page renders, and `add.pending` is `true` while `add` runs.")

        Heading("API services", id: "api-services").h2

        Markdown("When a service is called from more than one place, describe it once with `api`: where it is, how it is reached, and its endpoints. Every call is then typed, and the day the server changes its contract the build says so.")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

api Backend(base: "/api/v1") {
    timeout: 10.seconds
    retry: .backoff(times: 3, on: [.network, .timeout, .status5xx])

    get    users(page: Number = 1, q: String?) at "users" -> [User]!
    get    user(id: String) at "users/{id}" -> User!
    delete removeUser(id: String) at "users/{id}" -> !
}

page Users(path: "/users", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    state page: Number = 1
    resource rows = Backend.users(page: page, refresh: .onFocus)

    Heading("Users").h1
    match rows {
        .loading { Skeleton(height: "20px") }
        .failed(e) {
            match e {
                .offline(_) { Alert("Could not reach the server.").warning }
                .timeout(_) { Alert("The server took too long.").warning }
                else { Alert(e.message).danger }
            }
            Button("Try again") { on click { rows.reload() } }
        }
        .loaded(list) { for u in list by u.id { Text(u.name) } }
    }
    Button("Next page") { on click { page = page + 1 } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A `resource` over an endpoint takes the endpoint's type, so `u.nam` is `T05`. An endpoint the service does not have is `T06`. Every call goes through the engine `http` uses. It cancels a call when its page leaves, gives up after the `timeout:` (30 seconds unset), retries a request that is safe to send twice, keeps a `GET` answer as long as the server's `Cache-Control` allows, and makes one request for two readers of one address. The settings and their rules are in [Services and requests](/reference/requests).")

        Heading("Endpoints", id: "endpoints").h3

        Markdown("Each line after the settings is an endpoint:\n\n- **The verb** is the method: `get`, `post`, `put`, `patch`, `delete`.\n- **`at`** is where the server answers, below `base`. Every `\{name\}` in it is one of the endpoint's parameters (`N01`).\n- **Parameters** the path names go in the path. One called `body` is the request body. The rest go in the query string, or where `in header \"…\"` or `in cookie` says.\n- **A call** passes them by name, the first without its name if you like: `Backend.user(\"7\")`.\n- **`->`** says what a success returns and how a call fails: `-> T!` is a `T` or an `HttpError`, `-> T ! E` a `T` or a case of the error enum `E`, and `-> !` nothing, but it may fail.\n- A path parameter that is `null`, `\"\"`, `.` or `..` is not sent, since `users/` would be the whole collection. The call ends `.cancelled`, and a read over it stays `.loading` until the value arrives.\n\nA service's endpoints may be split into groups — `api Backend.todos \{ … \}`, called as `Backend.todos.list()`. A group sends what its service sends: its `base`, `timeout`, `retry` and `middleware`.")

        Heading("Error enums", id: "error-enums").h2

        Markdown("An endpoint that names an *error enum* turns each status the service answers with into a case of its own, carrying that status's body. One case, `http(error: HttpError)`, holds everything else:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type NewUser { name: String }
type FieldError { field: String, message: String }
type ValidationProblem = Problem { errors: [FieldError] }

/// Why adding someone can be refused.
enum CreateUserError {
    unprocessableContent(problem: ValidationProblem) on 422
    conflict(problem: Problem) on 409
    http(error: HttpError)
}

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    post createUser(body: NewUser) at "users" -> User ! CreateUserError
}

store People {
    state said = ""

    /// Every case handled where the call is made.
    action submit(name: String) {
        match await Backend.createUser(body: NewUser(name: name)) {
            .ok(u) { said = "Added {u.name}" }
            .err(.unprocessableContent(p)) { said = p.errors.map(e => e.message).join(", ") }
            .err(.conflict(_)) { said = "Already there" }
            .err(.http(.status(503, _))) { said = "The service is busy" }
            .err(.http(e)) { said = e.message }
        }
    }

    /// The failure passed up, as the action's own.
    action invite(name: String) -> User ! CreateUserError {
        let user = try await Backend.createUser(body: NewUser(name: name))
        return user
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **A case** names its status, `on 422` (or a class, `on 4XX`), and carries one value, the body. The `http` case is required, once (`N12`).\n- **A pattern** may look inside a case, `.err(.http(.status(503, _)))`.\n- **`try`** passes a failure up as the action's own, which must be able to hold it (`N03`): an `HttpError` goes into an error enum's `http` case.\n- **`.result`** is an action's last outcome, `null` before it has run: `if let .err(.conflict(p)) = People.invite.result \{ … \}` shows it.\n- **A `resource`** over such an endpoint has the enum in its `.failed(e)`.\n\n`HttpError` is what goes wrong between the page and the server:")

        Table(caption: "Say how a call fails") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Case").header
                    Table.Cell("When").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.offline(queued)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the request did not reach a server: no network, a server down, a CORS refusal; `queued` when a write was kept to send later") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.timeout(limit)`") }
                    Table.Cell("no answer within the timeout")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.cancelled`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the page left, the request changed, or `.cancel()`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.transport(message)`") }
                    Table.Cell("anything else that stopped it, such as a middleware that threw")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.decode(at, message)`") }
                    Table.Cell("the answer is not the declared type")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.status(code, problem)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the server answered with an error status; `problem` is its RFC 9457 body, if it sent one") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("Each has `.message`, for showing. `Problem` is RFC 9457's record — `type`, `title`, `status`, `detail`, `instance`, `extensions` — and a project extends it like any record, as `ValidationProblem` does.")

        Heading("Writes", id: "writes").h2

        Markdown("A write is a call made from an action or a handler, never a `resource`: a resource over a write is `N02`. A write's result must be heard — matched, or passed up with `try`. One awaited on its own is `N04`.\n\nAfter a write, the lists that show what it changed are out of date. `invalidates` names them, on the write. When the write succeeds, every resource on the page over one of them is refreshed, drawn in place:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String }
type NewTodo { title: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") { }

api Backend.todos {
    get   list() at "todos" -> [Todo]!
    get   byId(id: String) at "todos/{id}" -> Todo!
    post  create(body: NewTodo) at "todos" -> Todo!
        invalidates [list]
    delete remove(id: String) at "todos/{id}" -> !
        invalidates [list, byId(id)]
}

store Drafts {
    state said = ""
    action add(title: String) {
        let undo = Backend.todos.list.update(list => list.concat([Todo(id: "new", title: title)]))
        if let .err(e) = await Backend.todos.create(body: NewTodo(title: title)) {
            undo()
            said = e.message
        }
    }
}

page Todos(path: "/todos", title: "Todos", description: "The list.") {
    use Drafts
    resource todos = Backend.todos.list()
    Heading("Todos").h1
    Button("Add bread") { on click { Drafts.add("Bread") } }
    Text(Drafts.said)
    match todos {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { for todo in list by todo.id { Text(todo.title) } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- Each name in `invalidates` is a read of the same group, and each argument is one of the write's own parameters (`N05`).\n- `Backend.todos.list.update(change)` changes what every read of `list` shows, at once, and returns what takes it back. `Drafts.add` shows the new todo before the server answers, and takes it back on a refusal.")

        Heading("Optimistic updates", id: "optimistic-updates").h3

        Markdown("`optimistic(holder, change)` does the same for a state you hold yourself. If anything later in the action fails — a `try` passing a failure up, or a throw — the change is taken back:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Todo { id: String, title: String }
type Rename { title: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    patch rename(id: String, body: Rename) at "todos/{id}" -> Todo!
}

store Todos {
    state items: [Todo] = []

    action rename(id: String, title: String) -> ! {
        optimistic(items, list => list.map(x => if x.id == id { Todo(id: x.id, title: title) } else { x }))
        try await Backend.rename(id: id, body: Rename(title: title))
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The new title shows at once. If `rename` fails, `try` passes the failure up, and the old title comes back.")

        Heading("Pagination and streaming", id: "pagination-and-streaming").h2

        Heading("Pagination", id: "pagination").h3

        Markdown("How an endpoint pages is part of its contract, so you say it once, on the endpoint, with `pages`. A read of it then holds every page loaded so far:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Post { id: String, title: String }
type FeedPage { posts: [Post], nextCursor: String? }

api Social(base: "/api") {
    get feed(after: String?) at "feed" -> FeedPage!
        pages .cursor(param: after, next: p => p.nextCursor, items: p => p.posts)
}

page Feed(path: "/feed", title: "Feed", description: "What is new, a page at a time.") {
    resource feed = Social.feed()

    Heading("Feed").h1
    for post in feed.items by post.id { Text(post.title) }
    if feed.hasMore {
        Button("More", loading: feed.pending) { on click { feed.loadMore() } }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- `.items` is every item loaded so far, in order.\n- `.loadMore()` asks for the next page as a refresh: what is shown stays.\n- `.hasMore` is whether there is a next page.\n- `param:` names the parameter the next page goes in; `items:` says where a page's items are, when the page is not a list itself.\n\nThere are three ways to page:")

        Table(caption: "Load results a page at a time") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Written").header
                    Table.Cell("Asks for the next page with").header
                    Table.Cell("Stops when").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pages .number(param: page)`") }
                    Table.Cell("the parameter, one more each time")
                    Table.Cell("a page comes back empty")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pages .cursor(param: after, next: …)`") }
                    Table.Cell("the cursor the last page gave")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`next:` gives `null`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`pages .link`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the `Link` header's `rel=\"next\"` (RFC 8288)") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("no `Link` names one") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Streamed responses", id: "streamed-responses").h3

        Markdown("An endpoint that answers one JSON value a line — a log tail, a build's output — is marked `streamed`, and declares the list its lines make:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type LogLine { at: String, text: String }

api Deploys(base: "/api") {
    get tail(id: String) at "deploys/{id}/log" -> [LogLine]! streamed
}

store Follow {
    state last = ""
    action watch(id: String) {
        for await line in Deploys.tail(id: id) { last = line.text }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A `resource` over it is `.loaded` from the first line, and its list grows as the rest arrive. `for await line in …` runs its body for each line as it arrives. Each line is held to `LogLine`. `pages` and `streamed` are a read's: a write is sent once (`N14`).")

        Heading("Credentials and middleware", id: "credentials-and-middleware").h2

        Markdown("A session belongs in an httpOnly cookie, which no script on the page can read. `credentials: .sameOrigin` (or `.include`, for a service on another origin) sends it with every call, and the page holds no token at all.")

        Heading("Middleware", id: "middleware").h3

        Markdown("What every call carries — a token, the reader's language, a request ID — is *middleware*: steps each request passes through, in the order written in `middleware [ … ]`. This service refreshes the token on a `401`, sends it as a bearer token, and tags each request:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }
type Token { token: String }

store Session {
    state accessToken = ""
    action refresh() -> ! {
        let fresh: Token = try await http.post("/api/token")
        accessToken = fresh.token
    }
}

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    middleware [refreshOn401(Session.refresh), bearer(Session.accessToken), requestId]

    get me() at "me" -> User!
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`refreshOn401` goes before `bearer`, so the request sent again carries the new token. Requests that hear `401` at the same time share one run of the action. The other built-in steps are `header(name, value)` and `log`.\n\nA step of your own is a `middleware timed(req, next) \{ … \}` declaration that returns what `await next(req)` returns.")

        Heading("Cross-origin APIs", id: "cross-origin-apis").h3

        Markdown("A page on `https://example.com` that calls `https://api.example.com` makes a *cross-origin request*. The API must answer with CORS headers — `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` if cookies go with the call — or the browser refuses the answer. The page is not told why, so the call fails as `.offline`, and the console names CORS.\n\n`wf serve` has no proxy, so an API on `localhost:8080` must allow `localhost:3000` too. One origin for both, behind a reverse proxy, needs no CORS ([Serve the API on the same origin](/recipes/api-on-the-same-origin)). A build that ships a content security policy lists the API's origin in `meta.connect` ([Security](/about/security)).")

        Heading("OpenAPI", id: "openapi").h2

        Markdown("When the server publishes an OpenAPI spec (3.0 or 3.1, JSON or YAML), let it write the contract:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf api add openapi.json --name Backend"),
                Line(command: "wf api sync           # after the spec changes"),
                Line(command: "wf api check          # in CI: fails when the files are behind the spec")
            ])

        Markdown("`add` records the service in `webfluent.app.json`, writes `src/api/backend/service.wf` once for you to edit, and writes the contract into `src/api/backend/generated/`, which `sync` rewrites whole. Each tag is a group, each operation an endpoint named by its `operationId`, each schema a `type` or an `enum`, and each operation's error statuses the cases of an error enum. `wf check` warns `N07` when the files are behind the spec. Customise beside them, never inside: a `type` you declare wins over the spec's of the same name, an endpoint the spec lacks goes in a group of your own, and what a write makes stale and how a read pages go in `service.wf`:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Person { id: String, name: String }
type PersonPage { items: [Person], nextCursor: String? }

api Backend(base: "/api") {
    invalidations {
        people.deletePerson(id) invalidates [people.listPeople, people.getPerson(id)]
    }
    pagination {
        people.listPeople pages .cursor(param: after, next: p => p.nextCursor, items: p => p.items)
    }
}

// generated/people.wf, written by `wf api sync`
api Backend.people {
    get listPeople(after: String?) at "people" -> PersonPage!
    get getPerson(id: String) at "people/{id}" -> Person!
    delete deletePerson(id: String) at "people/{id}" -> !
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("For a prototype, `api Backend from \"openapi.json\" (base: \"/api\")` reads the same contract without writing files, and `wf api eject Backend` turns it into files later. [Call a service from its OpenAPI spec](/recipes/service-from-openapi) walks through one.")

        Heading("Testing with fakes", id: "testing-with-fakes").h2

        Markdown("A page that reads a service has nothing to show in a test until something answers. A `fake` answers a group's endpoints in place of the network:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Person { id: String, name: String }

api Backend(base: "/api") { }

api Backend.people {
    get list() at "people" -> [Person]!
}

page People(path: "/", title: "People", description: "The team.") {
    resource people = Backend.people.list()
    Heading("People").h1
    match people {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .failed(e) { Alert(e.message).danger }
        .loaded(list) { for p in list by p.id { Text(p.name) } }
    }
}

fake TwoPeople for Backend.people {
    list() { .ok([Person(id: "1", name: "Ada"), Person(id: "2", name: "Grace")]) }
}

test "lists the team"(fakes: [TwoPeople]) {
    People
    expect "Grace"
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- A fake is held to the group's contract (`N06`); one that answers `.err(.status(503, null))` tests a failure.\n- A test that names `fakes:` runs in a browser and sends nothing. A call no fake answers fails the test.\n- `fake Examples for Backend.people from examples` answers every endpoint it does not answer itself with the spec's example of a success.\n- `wf verify --fakes tests/fakes.wf` loads every page of the site with the fakes answering. A build leaves fakes out.\n\n[Testing](/learn/16-testing) covers tests; [Test a page with a fake service](/recipes/test-with-a-fake) tests a write.")

        Heading("Offline writes", id: "offline-writes").h2

        Markdown("With `offline` in the config, the build writes a service worker, and `\"sync\": true` keeps a write that failed for want of a network, to send when the connection returns:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"{ "offline": { "precache": ["/"], "sync": true } }"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("- Only a write safe to send twice is kept: a `PUT`, a `DELETE`, or a `POST` or `PATCH` whose endpoint is marked `idempotent`. Another `POST` may have reached the server, and sent again would be a second order, so it fails `.offline(queued: false)`.\n- A kept write's call fails `.offline(queued: true)`: no answer came, and the write waits, so the page should not show it as done.\n- `network.queued` counts what waits. Once a kept write is sent, what its endpoint `invalidates` is asked again.\n\nSee [Keep writes made offline](/recipes/keep-writes-made-offline) and [Make a site work offline](/recipes/work-offline).")

        Heading("Build-time data", id: "build-time-data").h2

        Markdown("For content known before the site is built — posts, a price list, a team page — `data name: T = \"file.json\"` reads a JSON file from the project directory or `src/` at build time. Its value is a constant, in scope in every page and component, and written into the pre-rendered page:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, date: String, body: String }

data posts: [Post] = "posts.json"

page Blog(path: "/", title: "Blog", description: "Every post.") {
    derived sorted = posts.sortBy(p => p.date).reverse()

    Heading("Blog").h1
    for p in sorted by p.slug {
        Card {
            Link(p.title, to: "/posts/{p.slug}")
            Text(format(p.date, .date)).muted.sm
        }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        DocsCodeBlock(##"[
  { "slug": "hello", "title": "Hello", "date": "2026-01-10", "body": "# Hi\nFirst post." },
  { "slug": "second", "title": "Second", "date": "2026-02-02", "body": "More." }
]"##, title: "json")

        Markdown("The build holds the file to the type. A record that is not a `Post` stops the build with `T33`, naming the file and the place: `$[3].date is \"March 5\", not a Date`.")

        Heading("A page per item", id: "a-page-per-item").h3

        Markdown("To give each post its own HTML file in a static build, list the values of the route parameter in `paths:`:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type Post { slug: String, title: String, date: String, body: String }

data posts: [Post] = "posts.json"

page PostPage(path: "/posts/:slug", slug: String, title: "Post", description: "A post.", paths: posts.map(p => p.slug)) {
    derived post = posts.find(p => p.slug == slug)
    if let p = post {
        Heading(p.title).h1
        Markdown(p.body)
    } else {
        Alert("No such post").warning
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The build writes `posts/hello/index.html` and `posts/second/index.html`, and lists each in the sitemap. `paths:` must be known at build time: a `data` file, a `const` or a literal. For a route with several parameters, each value is a map naming them. A single-page app answers every slug; only the static build needs the list.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Table(caption: "Troubleshooting") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What you see").header
                    Table.Cell("Why, and what to do").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The console says a response was blocked by CORS; the call fails `.offline`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The API is on another origin and does not allow yours ([Call an API on another origin](#cross-origin-apis)).") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.decode(at, message)`, such as `$[1].name is missing`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The server sent another shape than the declared type. Fix the server or the `type`.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A resource stays `.loading`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A path parameter is `null` or `\"\"`, so the call is not sent. It goes out when the value arrives.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The page goes back to `.loading` when state changes") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The address now names something else, so it is a first load. `placeholder: .previous` keeps the last value.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A list does not show what a write added")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The write does not name the read in `invalidates`, or it failed.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A post's page is missing from a static build")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Its value is not in `paths:`.") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Quiz", id: "quiz").h2

        Quiz("A resource with `refresh: .every(30.seconds)` asks again while its list is on screen. What does its `match` show while that refresh runs?", id: "Fetching data#16", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loading` arm, then the new list", right: false, why: "Only a first load, or a request whose address changed, shows `.loading`."),
            QuizChoice(text: "An empty list until the answer arrives", right: false, why: "The value stays while a refresh runs."),
            QuizChoice(text: "The `.loaded` arm with the old list, then the answer drawn in place", right: true, why: "A refresh stays `.loaded`, with `refreshing` true; rows whose key is still there keep their nodes.")
        ])

        Quiz("`post order(body: Order) at \"orders\" -> Order!` times out. What does the engine do, with `retry: .backoff(times: 3)` on the service?", id: "Fetching data#16", choices: [
            QuizChoice(text: "It sends the order again, up to three times", right: false, why: "A `POST` that timed out may have reached the server; sent again it could be a second order."),
            QuizChoice(text: "It fails with `.timeout`, and sends nothing again", right: true, why: "Only a request safe to send twice is retried. Mark the endpoint `idempotent` and it is retried with one `Idempotency-Key`."),
            QuizChoice(text: "It keeps the order to send when the network returns", right: false, why: "A timeout is not the network going away, and a `POST` that is not `idempotent` is never kept.")
        ])

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("**A `match` with no `.failed` arm.** When the request fails, no arm draws, and the reader is not told:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

page P(path: "/", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    resource users: [User] = http.get("/api/users")
    Heading("Users").h1
    match users {
        .loading { Spinner }
        .loaded(list) { Text("{list.length} users") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf", expect: "T21")

        Markdown("Add `.failed(e) \{ Alert(e.message).danger \}`, or an `else` ([T21](/errors/T21)).\n\n**Reading `.value` as if it had arrived.** Before the first answer, `name.value` is `null`:")

        DocsCodeBlock(#"type User { id: String, name: String }

page P(path: "/", title: "Users", description: "Everyone.") {
    resource users: [User] = http.get("/api/users")
    Heading("Users").h1
    Text("{users.value.length} users")
}"#, title: "wf", expect: "T04")

        Markdown("Read it through `?.` with a fallback, `\{users.value?.length ?? 0\}`, or show it inside the `.loaded` arm, where the binding is never null ([T04](/errors/T04)).")
    }
}
